{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git show HEAD:docs/helix/02-design/contracts/CONTRACT-003-ddx-agent-service.md | nl -ba | sed -n '360,460p'\"","aggregated_output":"   360\t  \"routing_actual\": {\n   361\t    \"harness\": \"agent\",\n   362\t    \"provider\": \"openrouter\",   // distinct from start-event routing_decision when fallback fired\n   363\t    \"model\": \"qwen/qwen3.6\",\n   364\t    \"fallback_chain_fired\": [\"bragi:qwen/qwen3.6 (timeout)\", \"openrouter:qwen/qwen3.6 (success)\"]\n   365\t  }\n   366\t}\n   367\t```\n   368\t\n   369\t## Test seam types\n   370\t\n   371\t```go\n   372\t// FakeProvider supports three patterns:\n   373\t//   - Static script: sequence of pre-recorded responses, consumed in order.\n   374\t//   - Dynamic callback: function called per request returning a response.\n   375\t//   - Error injection: per-call status override.\n   376\ttype FakeProvider struct {\n   377\t    Static      []FakeResponse                            // for static script pattern\n   378\t    Dynamic     func(req FakeRequest) (FakeResponse, error)  // for dynamic per-call pattern\n   379\t    InjectError func(callIndex int) error                 // for error injection pattern\n   380\t}\n   381\t\n   382\ttype FakeRequest struct {\n   383\t    Messages []Message\n   384\t    Tools    []string\n   385\t    Model    string\n   386\t}\n   387\t\n   388\ttype FakeResponse struct {\n   389\t    Text      string\n   390\t    ToolCalls []ToolCall\n   391\t    Usage     TokenUsage\n   392\t    Status    string  // \"success\" by default\n   393\t}\n   394\t\n   395\t// PromptAssertionHook is called once per Execute, with the system+user prompt\n   396\t// the agent actually sent to the model. Used by tests that verify prompt\n   397\t// construction/compaction without running a real provider.\n   398\ttype PromptAssertionHook func(systemPrompt, userPrompt string, contextFiles []string)\n   399\t\n   400\t// CompactionAssertionHook is called whenever a real compaction runs. No-op\n   401\t// compactions are NOT delivered (they don't fire compaction events either).\n   402\ttype CompactionAssertionHook func(messagesBefore, messagesAfter int, tokensFreed int)\n   403\t\n   404\t// ToolWiringHook is called once per Execute, with the resolved tool list and\n   405\t// the harness that received it. Used by tests that verify the right tools\n   406\t// land at the right harness given the request's permission level.\n   407\ttype ToolWiringHook func(harness string, toolNames []string)\n   408\t```\n   409\t\n   410\t## Reasoning contract\n   411\t\n   412\t`Reasoning` is the only preferred public control for model-side reasoning.\n   413\tConsumers do not set separate public thinking, effort, level, or budget fields.\n   414\tThe scalar accepts named values (`auto`, `off`, `low`, `medium`, `high`) and\n   415\tprovider/harness-supported extended values such as `minimal`, `xhigh` /\n   416\t`x-high`, and `max`. It also accepts numeric values through\n   417\t`ReasoningTokens(n)`, where `0` means explicit off and positive integers mean\n   418\tan explicit max reasoning-token budget or documented provider-equivalent\n   419\tnumeric value.\n   420\t\n   421\tNormalization is tri-state:\n   422\t\n   423\t- Empty means no caller preference.\n   424\t- `auto` means resolve model, catalog, or provider defaults.\n   425\t- `off`, `none`, `false`, and `0` mean explicit reasoning off.\n   426\t- Positive integers mean an explicit numeric request.\n   427\t\n   428\tDefault portable named-to-token budgets are `low=2048`, `medium=8192`, and\n   429\t`high=32768` only when a selected provider/model does not publish a more\n   430\tspecific map. Providers and subprocess harnesses may map resolved reasoning to\n   431\twire or CLI knobs named `reasoning`, `thinking`, `effort`, `variant`, or a\n   432\tnumeric budget. They may also drop auto/default reasoning controls for models\n   433\tthat do not support explicit reasoning control. Explicit unsupported values,\n   434\tunknown extended values, and over-limit numeric values fail clearly.\n   435\t\n   436\tCatalog tier defaults are part of route resolution: below-smart tiers\n   437\t(`cheap`, `fast`, `standard`, `code-economy`, and `code-medium`) default to\n   438\t`reasoning=off`, including local/economy Qwen targets. `smart` and `code-high`\n   439\tdefault to `reasoning=high`. Any explicit caller `Reasoning` value wins over\n   440\tthese defaults, including supported values above high such as `xhigh` or\n   441\t`max`, and numeric values.\n   442\t\n   443\t## Sampling contract\n   444\t\n   445\t`ExecuteRequest.Temperature` and `ExecuteRequest.Seed` are the portable\n   446\tsampling controls. `Temperature=0` requests deterministic sampling. `Seed=0`\n   447\tmeans unset and lets the provider choose. Native OpenAI-compatible providers\n   448\thonor both fields. Providers or subprocess harnesses that do not expose an\n   449\tequivalent seed control may ignore `Seed`; callers that require strict parity\n   450\tmust treat those runs as advisory/non-deterministic.\n   451\t\n   452\t## Bead Execution Policy\n   453\t\n   454\tDDx bead implementation should use a two-pass policy against this service:\n   455\ttry a cheap or standard profile with `reasoning=off` first, then escalate only\n   456\twhen the first pass produced evidence that a smarter model is likely to help.\n   457\tThe initial pass should use `ModelRef=cheap` or `ModelRef=standard` with an\n   458\texplicit `ReasoningOff` request so local/economy models do not spend\n   459\treasoning tokens by default.\n   460\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
